    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new `deduplicateCustomerCode` slots naturally into this codebase:\n\n- **Right unit / placement.** It's a private helper on the same service that already owns customer-code generation, called from `generateCustomerCode` right where the base code is built. Splitting the \"compute base\" and \"make unique\" steps into two clearly-named methods reads well.\n- **Idiomatic for this file.** It mirrors the existing sibling helpers (`countNamesakes`, `assertIdentityUnique`, `assertHouseholdUnique`) exactly: iterate `ownerRepository.findAll()`, exclude the owner being saved via `!existing.getId().equals(owner.getId())`, and guard against `null`. A maintainer here would find nothing surprising \u2014 the in-memory `findAll()` scan is the established convention in this class, not a new pattern this change introduces.\n- **No duplication or catch-all creep.** No logic is repeated within the change, and the class isn't taking on a new responsibility \u2014 customer-code assignment already lives here.\n- **Documentation matches convention.** The Javadoc is at the same level of detail as its neighbors, including the rationale for the self-exclusion.\n\nStructure is clean and additive; nothing to change.",
